  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:51:26.293Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nA minimum-wage earner with a single employer is not required to file an annual income tax return if their income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer. This is due to the substituted filing provision under the NIRC.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provision is found in Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, where the income tax has been withheld correctly, are not required to file an annual income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A of the NIRC allows for substituted filing of income tax returns for employees whose income tax has been withheld at source by their employer. This means that if the tax withheld equals the tax due, the employee does not need to file a separate return; the employer's withholding certificate serves as the tax return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor minimum-wage earners employed by a single employer, this provision simplifies their tax obligations. They can rely on their employer to handle tax withholding, eliminating the need for additional paperwork and filing, provided that the withholding is accurate and complete. This reduces the administrative burden on low-income earners and ensures compliance with tax obligations without the need for individual filings.",
